The data-plane and OSPF control-plane are currently healthy. From host_1_1_1_1, ICMP to all data‑center servers in 10.200.0.0/24 succeeds (0% loss to 10.200.0.3–.6 and to 10.200.0.2), and HTTP to 10.200.0.3 returns promptly. This proves end‑to‑end reachability from the user VLAN to the server farm. On the control side, FRR OSPF adjacencies are Full across the backbone and distribution: router_core_1 has Full neighbors on eth0/eth1/eth2, router_core_2 and router_core_3 each show Full/Backup or Full/DR states on their core links, and the switch_server_access neighbor relationship to router_core_3 is Full. With adjacencies established and data-plane reachability verified, there is no evidence of a current OSPF failure or a crashed router_core_3 process.

Mechanism-wise, if OSPF had been down or router_core_3 unreachable, host_1_1_1_1 would lack routes to 10.200.0.0/24 and pings/HTTP would fail—this is not observed. Localization confirms FRR OSPF on router_core_3 and its peers is operating correctly.

Root cause: No active fault detected; the reported outage was likely a transient during the maintenance window that has since cleared.

Corrective fix: No restart is required at this time. Recommend leaving devices as-is and monitoring OSPF for flaps; if issues recur, review maintenance/change logs and consider checking/restarting FRR on router_core_3, but it is not warranted now.
